```c
sem_t empty, full;      // counting semaphores
pthread_mutex_t mutex;  // protects the buffer itself
int buffer[N];

void init() {
    sem_init(&empty, 0, N);   // N slots free
    sem_init(&full, 0, 0);    // 0 slots filled
}

void producer(int item) {
    sem_wait(&empty);            // wait for a free slot
    pthread_mutex_lock(&mutex);
    buffer_put(item);
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&mutex);
    sem_post(&full);             // signal a filled slot
}

void consumer() {
    sem_wait(&full);              // wait for a filled slot
    pthread_mutex_lock(&mutex);
    int item = buffer_get();
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&mutex);
    sem_post(&empty);             // signal a free slot
}
```

Three synchronization objects, three jobs: `empty` and `full` count available slots in each direction (this is signaling), while `mutex` protects the buffer's internal pointers from a torn read/write (this is mutual exclusion). Mixing these roles up — using one semaphore for both — is a common source of subtle bugs.
